# Vedaai grader — configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, so never
# put a real secret here.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Loaded by the API with:  uv run --env-file ../../.env uvicorn grader.main:app
# The web app reads only NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE, from apps/web/.env.local.
#
# Nothing here is needed to run the pipeline locally against the sample files.
# Question extraction, answer mapping and highlighting work with no credentials at
# all; these switch on the hosted services.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Handwriting recognition — AWS Textract
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# NOT an API key. Textract authenticates with IAM, so locally it needs an access
# key pair, and on ECS it needs none at all — the task role supplies credentials
# and these three lines stay empty in the deployed environment.
#
# The IAM user or role needs exactly one permission: textract:DetectDocumentText.
#
# Why Textract over the alternatives, given the app is going on AWS: it returns
# bounding boxes already normalized to [0,1] with the origin top-left, which is
# this project's coordinate contract exactly, so no conversion can drift. It also
# keeps the recognizer in the same region as the service, so page images never
# leave the VPC and there is no egress bill.

AWS_REGION=ap-south-1
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Which engine reads handwriting. `textract` for anything deployed; `paddle` runs
# the local model instead, which needs no credentials but downloads ~600 MB of
# weights and takes about 14s per page on a laptop CPU.
#
# A typed question paper never reaches either of these — it is read from its own
# PDF text layer, which is exact and free. This setting only affects handwriting.
OCR_ENGINE=textract


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Page image storage — S3
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Rendered page bitmaps are written here and served back to the browser. They are
# content-addressed, so one question paper shared across a class is rendered once.
#
# Needs s3:PutObject and s3:GetObject on the bucket. Set a lifecycle rule to
# expire objects — these are student answer scripts, they are regenerable from the
# upload, and there is no reason to keep them.
#
# Leave S3_PAGE_BUCKET empty to keep writing to local disk instead, which is what
# you want for development.

S3_PAGE_BUCKET=
S3_PAGE_PREFIX=pages/

# Local fallback, used only when S3_PAGE_BUCKET is empty.
PAGE_STORE_ROOT=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Marking — Anthropic
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Optional, and the app is honest without it: marking still returns the rubric
# derived from the paper and the located answer, with every point left for the
# teacher. It proposes no score rather than inventing one.
#
# With a key set, each mark cites the specific lines that earned it, and those
# citations are validated before anything is shown — an unresolvable citation
# invalidates the grade rather than being quietly dropped.

ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Overridable so a deployment can pin a version. Defaults to claude-sonnet-5.
GRADER_MODEL=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Wiring the two services together
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The browser talks to the API directly — page images, the progress stream, and
# the mapping edits all go straight to it — so the web origin has to be allowed
# by name. Comma-separated.
#
# Leave empty in development and any loopback origin is accepted on any port.
# Setting it switches that allowance off, which is what you want in production.
WEB_ORIGINS=

# Where the browser should find the API. Baked into the frontend at build time, so
# it belongs in apps/web/.env.local rather than here — repeated for reference.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=https://api.your-domain.example
